    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "\"Our number system (called the 'position system') has used the zero for a long time, but only in the 16th century (Cardano, Tartaglia) is the zero for the first time not considered as nothing, but as a number, as numerical reality. And only now in the 20th century, the Square is recognized as a plastic value, as the zero in the complex body of art. This fullcolored Square, stamped out completely continuously with color in a white plane, has now started to build a new space.\"",
      "links": []
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "El Lissitzky: \"Kunst und Pangeometrie.\"\n\nIn: C. Einstein & P. Westheim (eds.): Europa Almanach. Potsdam: Gustav Kiepenheuer, 1925.",
      "links": []
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "\"For art should not proceed towards reduction or simplification, but towards complexity.\"",
      "links": []
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Kazimir Malevich: Ot kubizma i futurizma do suprematizma. Novy zhivopisny realizm. Third edition. Moscow, 1916.\n\nEnglish translation: \"From Cubism and Futurism to Suprematism. The New Realism in Painting.\" In: K.S. Malevich: Essays on Art, Vol.1. 1915-1928. Copenhagen: Borgen, 1971, pp. 19-41 [p.22].",
      "links": []
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "A slightly more detailed discussion concerning the suprematist algebra",
