    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Kazimir Malevich: White on White (1918)",
      "links": [],
      "level": 4
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "src": "/images/nothing/monochrome/malevich-rodchenko/malevitch-SCWhOnWh1918-M.jpg",
      "alt": "",
      "caption": "",
      "width": "250",
      "height": "250",
      "links": []
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "In \"Suprematist Composition: White on White\" (1918), Malevich\ndisplays a white square in a white space. This piece is obviously not an empty,monochrome\npainting – but it is difficult to resist the idea\nthat it depicts such a monochrome. For Malevich, white was the\ncolor of space – he said this with so many words, and all suprematist\npaintings show it. The white square in \"White on White\" is\nthus an image of the very space it inhabits.\n\n\n\n\"White on White\" is an early example of a self-referential\npiece – akin to Nam June Paik's \"TV Buddha\", various\npieces by Maurits Escher, and Pistoletto's \"Infinity\".",
      "links": []
    },
    {
