    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Aleksandr Rodchenko: Pure Color, 1921",
      "links": [],
      "level": 4
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "The first non-figurative monochromes were painted by Aleksandr Rodchenko in 1921. Along with two other paintings by Rodchenko (\"Line\" and \"Cell\"), they were exhibited in September 1921 in the first installment of the two-part exhibition 5x5=25 in Moscow. The other participants were Varvara Stepanova, Aleksandra Ekster, Liubov Popova, and Aleksandr Vesnin.",
      "links": []
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "src": "/images/nothing/monochrome/malevich-rodchenko/rodchenko-RodchenkoRed.jpg",
      "alt": "",
      "caption": "",
      "width": "166",
      "height": "197",
      "links": []
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Pure Red Color\n\n(Chistyi krasnyi tsvet)",
      "links": []
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "src": "/images/nothing/monochrome/malevich-rodchenko/rodchenko-RodchenkoBlue.jpg",
      "alt": "",
      "caption": "",
      "width": "166",
      "height": "197",
      "links": []
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Pure Blue Color\n\n(Chistyi sinii tsvet)",
      "links": []
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "src": "/images/nothing/monochrome/malevich-rodchenko/rodchenko-RodchenkoYellow.jpg",
      "alt": "",
      "caption": "",
      "width": "166",
      "height": "197",
      "links": []
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Pure Yellow Color\n\n(Chistyi zheltyi tsvet)",
      "links": []
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "\"From here, Constructivism proceeds to the negation of all art in its entirety, and calls into question the necessity of a specific activity of art for the creation of a universal aesthetic.\"",
      "links": []
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Varvara Stepanova: Lecture on Constructivism, 22 December 1921.\n\nIn: Peter Noever: Aleksandr M. Rodchenko - Varvara F. Stepanova. The Future Is Our Only Goal.\n\nMunich: Prestel, 1991, pp. 174-178.",
      "links": []
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "\"I reduced painting to its logical conclusion and exhibited three canvases: red, blue and yellow. I affirmed: it's all over. Basic colors. Every plane is a plane and there is to be no representation.\"",
      "links": []
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Aleksandr Rodchenko: Working with Majakovskii. Ms., 1939.",
      "links": []
    },
